00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:52.925
So New York City
Cyber Command, NYC3--

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:54.050
Doing Right by New Yorkers.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:56.930
Number 1, we're a
new government agency

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:00.800
charged with defending New
York City and the city of New

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.960
York from cyber threats
and providing tools

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.180
so that residents,
visitors, and businesses can

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.070
lead safer lives online.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.850
We have a dedicated team of
cybersecurity and technology

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:12.710
professionals that
monitor city systems

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:16.130
24/7, 365 in collaboration
with other city

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.350
agencies such as
Department of Information

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.690
Technology and
telecommunications

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:22.910
and other stakeholders
to ensure our cities

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:26.670
are safe and resilient
from cyber threats.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.910
And number 3, we believe
that cybersecurity

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:30.020
is a public safety issue.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:33.020
On behalf of city government
and this administration,

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:34.610
we lead the NYC
secure initiative,

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.610
which is a policy position
that says cybersecurity

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.350
is not only a public
safety issue but one

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.720
in which your government
owes you something.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:44.060
It owes you actual
tools that you can use

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:46.880
to lead safer digital lives.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.820
We have three tactics
under this policy.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:54.560
Number 1 is an enterprise
grade mobile threat detection

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.630
app called the NYC Secure App
available on the iOS in Android

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:00.260
app stores.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.410
You can just-- or
Google NYC secure app.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:07.820
And we believe that
security and privacy are not

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:09.230
mutually exclusive.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.360
Our mobile threat
detection app does

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.370
all of its work on
the mobile phone.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:15.350
It doesn't send anything
outside of the phone.

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:16.370
It collects an OPII.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.170
It doesn't even have the rights
to PII on your mobile device.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:22.310
Two, we partnered with
a nonprofit organization

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:25.310
called Quad 9, which offers
free privacy respecting

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.500
recursive DNS filtering,
and we're rolling that out

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.420
to all free public
Wi-Fi networks

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:30.770
by the end of this year.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.260
That's because we believe that
residents who use our Wi-Fi

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:36.470
should not be victimized
as they do so.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:39.200
And number 3, those of you that
saw in the Wall Street Journal

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:41.930
last Thursday, our agency
and my boss Jeff Brown

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:46.100
partnered with the Manhattan DA,
Cy Vance, and the commissioner

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.620
of the New York City Police
Department, James O'Neill,

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.440
on cyber critical services
and infrastructure.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:55.490
It is a local cybersecurity
critical infrastructure

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.950
focused organization
designed to break down

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.310
barriers between
organizations across verticals

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:04.845
and be selfless and ruthless
about defending the city of New

00:07:04.845 --> 00:07:05.970
York against cyber threats.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:10.620
So very briefly, that's
the executive order

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:14.040
that created us in July of
2017, executive order 28

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:17.370
signed by Bill de Blasio.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:21.480
And broadly speaking, we wanted
to undertake a very substantial

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.740
problem in a new way.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:26.490
And we have three
goals at a high level.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:31.440
One is build a secure
cloud-based security log

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.680
aggregation platform
for city systems, Two,

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.740
enable via this platform
alerting visualization

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:41.730
and analytics for cybersecurity
professionals, both analytics

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:45.390
that we had with existing
vendor partner relationships,

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:48.000
capabilities that we might
acquire in the future,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.240
and some capabilities
that we develop in-house.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.060
And three-- and maybe
most philosophically--

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:58.080
we want to scale non linearly
with two kinds of demands

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.300
or two kinds of
issues that we face.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:03.670
Number 1, demand on city
services that is delivered by,

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:07.410
with, and through a technology
system grows every single day.

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:10.440
In addition to 3/16
public IP addresses,

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.920
we have over 400,000 workstation
servers and mobile devices

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:14.970
that are city owned.

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:18.690
We have another half a million
or so connected devices ranging

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:22.290
from water meters
to the GPS devices

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:24.748
on the snowplows that enable
the app to tell you when

00:08:24.748 --> 00:08:26.040
it's going to plow your street.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.440
So this is a really, really,
really big and complicated

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:29.430
infrastructure.

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:32.220
And each one of those devices
in some way large or small

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:34.260
expands the threat surface.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:38.679
And for those of you that saw
Mike Aiello's talk yesterday,

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:41.340
we want to reduce
that threat surface

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:44.640
to a meaningfully small area
that we as defenders can

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.290
interact with.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:50.250
And additionally, the threats
against any one of those points

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.110
increases every single day.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:54.990
Five years ago for
you to take advantage

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:58.170
of the most advanced
hacking tools allegedly made

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:00.060
available by the
National Security

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.565
Agency and other governments
around the world,

00:09:02.565 --> 00:09:05.300
you would have to work
for those agencies.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:09.870
If those of you that remember
the eternal blue [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:14.207
these are leveraging alleged
NSA type capabilities,

00:09:14.207 --> 00:09:16.290
and you could today right
now pull out your laptop

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:19.020
and go to GitHub and have
the power of the NSA.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:22.500
So that is a fundamentally
categorically different world

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.200
than even three
or five years ago,

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.250
and we need to be able
to respond to that there.

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:31.600
And we think that our
data pipeline does that.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.030
To do that, we have a
number of core beliefs.

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:36.920
One is security.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:40.210
The ability to do comprehensive
retrospective search

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:43.330
on our security telemetry
in a separate environment

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:46.120
fundamentally changes
the strategic dynamic

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:47.740
between us and our adversaries.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:49.780
It enables detection
based on a variety

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.690
of sources with a variety
of partners in a secure way.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:54.910
It can guide detection
and response efforts.

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:56.830
It can lead to faster
remediation and faster

00:09:56.830 --> 00:09:59.890
detection and over time
allow us to leverage

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:03.050
a key asset, our data, in
order to become predictive,

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:05.750
and we'll talk about where
we think that's the case.

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:09.100
Additionally, we want our
confidence in the team,

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:11.465
confidence in the tools,
and confidence in the plan.

00:10:11.465 --> 00:10:13.840
So we're-- obviously I spoke
about the incredible team we

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:15.340
have in New York
City Cyber Command.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:18.360
I'm going to talk about just
one of the tools we have

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:23.050
and going forward the future,
zero trust networking, our plan

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:26.770
for fundamentally changing
the battlespace with regards

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:28.360
to cyber threats.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:29.380
Two, flexibility.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:31.520
Cyber is a team sport.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.120
We need a security product
agnostic environment

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:36.820
that can allow multiple tools
to operate either concurrently

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:38.290
or in parallel.

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:40.990
We want to be able to
test, add new capabilities,

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:42.580
remove them rapidly.

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:45.940
We want to be data centric,
not product centric.

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.520
And broadly speaking we
want to move from a request

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:51.000
and response
framework to a publish

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.570
and subscribe framework.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:54.790
And that's one that enables
the future of things

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:57.220
like machine learning,
things like automation.

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:59.830
Additionally, we need to build
federation and multi-tenancy

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:00.790
from the ground up.

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:02.260
We need to leverage metadata.

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:05.170
We need to apply this metadata
so that we can be fast.

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:08.320
This is a problem that has
to be done at machine speed,

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:11.440
not one at people speed.

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.600
Two, data centric.

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:15.250
What is data centric mean?

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:17.950
At some level, it's
about decision advantage.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:22.000
We want to provide our network
operators and defenders

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:25.370
with decision advantage
over their adversary

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:27.610
because we want our defenders
and network operators

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:30.920
to be able to know and do more
faster than the adversary.

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:32.920
And you can't hire your
way out of that problem.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:35.710
Obviously it starts
with a fantastic team,

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:37.810
but that's not where it ends.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:41.020
It is about having the right
data to the right analyst

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:42.730
or analytical process
at the right time

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:44.740
with the right context.

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:46.630
You need context not
to negatively impact

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.670
business operations,
and you can't do that

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:51.994
without data that is trusted,
reliable, and verifiable.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:12:00.160
So to restate that problem in
a slightly different way, each

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:02.770
and every day, we're asking
IT professionals and security

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:04.660
teams to make decisions.

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:06.610
Whether to take
an asset offline,

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:10.190
whether to remove access
to a system from some user,

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:11.920
they are making a trade.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:14.410
They are reducing some
risk, which might be present

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:17.517
in a system or in the future
via the action of an adversary,

00:12:17.517 --> 00:12:19.600
but they're degrading the
usability of that system

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:22.240
in some fundamental way.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:26.890
We want this process to happen
as rapidly and effectively

00:12:26.890 --> 00:12:28.420
as possible--

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:31.120
right time, right
data, right analyst.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:33.070
And what we face
today is not just

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:36.520
database access, insider
threat, SQL injection,

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:38.390
all these other things.

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:42.010
It is the compilation
of the interaction

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:44.530
of these forces within
a situation of extremely

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:45.970
high uncertainty.

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:48.760
If anybody's ever
remediated a security

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.640
or a technical incident
of extreme complexity,

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:53.770
you know that when the
incident first starts,

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:56.380
you can even be sure
of what you know.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:13:00.010
And what we're demonstrating
with this pipeline is

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:02.890
that we are removing-- we
are systematically removing

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:06.590
uncertainty from these
decisions for our consumers,

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:09.940
our consumers being the
security operation center

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:12.940
professionals that 24/7,
365 defend the city

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:15.530
from cyber threats
and the network

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:17.888
and IT operations
professionals which run

00:13:17.888 --> 00:13:19.180
those systems every single day.

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:25.330
So as a extension
of that metaphor,

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:28.510
we need to systematically
apply insight.

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:30.460
People, the term
machine learning,

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:34.030
and advanced automation,
people don't actually

00:13:34.030 --> 00:13:35.420
want machine learning.

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:36.370
They want learning.

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:38.380
They don't want
advanced automation.

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:40.910
They want the right thing
done as fast as possible.

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:45.700
So what our platform
enables is those workflows

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.693
to be systematically understood,
to be programmatically applied,

00:13:48.693 --> 00:13:49.860
and to be repeated at scale.

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:57.110
And like all organizations
like all projects of this scale

00:13:57.110 --> 00:14:00.240
and complexity, there's
a number of challenges.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:03.530
One is the scale volume and
federation as I intimated.

00:14:03.530 --> 00:14:06.160
The city is an organization of--

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:09.500
it has over 330,000
employees, as I

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:13.310
said 400,000 end points, a total
of about a million systems.

00:14:13.310 --> 00:14:15.320
There's hundreds
agencies ranging

00:14:15.320 --> 00:14:18.950
from the absolutely incredible
public safety professionals

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:21.840
of the NYPD and FDNY patrolling
the streets every single day

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:23.840
to the mayor's office of
Immigrant Affairs doing

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:26.060
absolutely critical cutting
edge work in support

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:31.310
of at risk communities,
50,000 police officers, much

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:34.760
much, much less than that,
immigration and civil service

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:35.810
professionals.

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:37.850
So that scale volume
and federation

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:40.640
makes it a very large
complex organization.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:43.290
Two, as I mentioned, the threat
surface and attack surface

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:44.450
is growing.

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:46.400
Three, I mentioned why
platform approaches

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:49.700
for some-- for an organization
like ours are not sufficient.

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:53.240
And while they might
be sufficient for some,

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:55.820
an entity of our size
and sophistication

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:58.290
is never going to be satisfied
with a single vendor.

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:00.110
And number 4 is technical debt.

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:03.440
The city has been doing
computers since the mid 60s.

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:05.067
The city has been
offering services

00:15:05.067 --> 00:15:06.650
to residents, visitors,
and businesses

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:10.770
with mainframes since the
days of the IBM System/360.

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:14.960
So this is an organization which
has a long and storied history

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:17.450
of using computing to
further the public good,

00:15:17.450 --> 00:15:19.550
and that means that we
must approach our legacy

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:22.280
systems with data,
not wishful thinking.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:28.710
Again how do we deal with
more data, more formats,

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:30.720
and more locations?

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.760
More formats and
more data obviously,

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:35.010
I talked about all
these systems I

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:37.050
mentioned throw of
data, large and small.

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:40.890
More formats, it's not just a
sys log feed over UDP anymore.

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:42.413
We have API-based access.

00:15:42.413 --> 00:15:44.580
We have platform and software
as a service offerings

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:45.980
that the city uses.

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:48.690
And more locations, the city
has a hybrid cloud strategy

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:50.820
like many organizations,
so it's not just

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:51.880
on premise data centers.

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:53.683
It's not just one
public cloud provider.

00:15:53.683 --> 00:15:54.600
It's all of the above.

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:00.060
So with all that
really as background,

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:05.130
our approach for security
data is, one, cloud native.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:09.060
We are built primarily on
Apache Beam and open source data

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:12.300
programming model and
the TLDR of Apache Beam,

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:15.480
it is highly parallel--
so it's fast--

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:18.450
and you can apply the
same data transformations

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:21.645
both on batch and in stream.

00:16:21.645 --> 00:16:24.810
And what that means is for
a small dedicated growing

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:28.410
organization like ourselves,
we can spend more time

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:31.005
understating our data and
transforming it in a way

00:16:31.005 --> 00:16:32.130
that our consumers refine--

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:34.680
will find meaningful than on
either running infrastructure

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:37.860
because we leverage Cloud
Dataflow from Google

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:43.270
than on the deep technical
underpinnings of a data

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:46.480
management model itself.

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:51.340
Two is zero trust, zero touch.

00:16:51.340 --> 00:16:54.520
We believe that the
BeyondCorp architecture,

00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:57.190
obviously spearheaded by
Google and now embraced

00:16:57.190 --> 00:17:01.330
by others in the BeyondCorp
alliance announced yesterday,

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:03.910
is the architecture
of the future.

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:06.520
Briefly, BeyondCorp
for zero trust

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:09.520
is removing authorization
and access decisions

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:12.550
from single points and
making them compilations

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:14.920
of decisions either about
what you know about a user,

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:18.609
what you know about a system, or
some combination of the above.

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:21.310
And zero touch, utilizing
infrastructure as code,

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:26.440
enables us to scale rapidly
elastically and apply

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:27.690
best practices.

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:29.440
And we're not just
security professionals.

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:32.020
We're also evangelists,
so this is an opportunity

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:36.100
through this project to eat
our own dog food with regards

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:37.840
to BeyondCorp and
zero touch that we

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:41.890
can bring to city agencies
along with the security

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:45.010
outcomes we deliver
to our stakeholders.

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:46.480
Number 3, modularity.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:48.045
It's a flexible architecture.

00:17:48.045 --> 00:17:49.420
One of the key
differentiators is

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:51.753
that this is a publish and
subscribe architecture rather

00:17:51.753 --> 00:17:53.080
than a request and response.

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:55.900
So that means we can take things
out and add them very rapidly.

00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:57.942
The fact that we combine
this with infrastructure

00:17:57.942 --> 00:18:00.950
as code increases our
velocity and our reliability

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:01.780
so that's all--

00:18:01.780 --> 00:18:04.300
those are things that
are worth focusing on.

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:05.410
And it's fast.

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:07.588
We intentionally
built this to be fast.

00:18:07.588 --> 00:18:09.130
And I think the next
slide I actually

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:11.350
have some of the metrics.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:16.210
Because if it is fast, you can
apply advanced things later.

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:18.670
But if it starts out
and it's not fast,

00:18:18.670 --> 00:18:20.660
it's very hard to
make it fast later.

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:24.280
So we built it
with speed in mind.

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:29.430
Obviously we use
the best technology,

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:31.860
the right open source projects,
the right architecture,

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:33.530
and we'll talk
about why that is.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:37.480
But we believe that to be-- this
is a machine speed problem, not

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:39.110
a human speed problem.

00:18:39.110 --> 00:18:42.100
So we want to counter our
latency in milliseconds and not

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:43.660
seconds.

00:18:43.660 --> 00:18:45.190
How many milliseconds?

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:48.640
So we on the average day
process billions and billions

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:49.140
of events.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:51.140
Actually yesterday I think
we processed slightly

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:53.195
over 12 billion events.

00:18:53.195 --> 00:18:54.570
And with-- the
average processing

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:56.610
time is less than
10 milliseconds.

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:58.920
That's to do-- over the
next couple of slides,

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:02.010
I'll show you all of the things
we're doing in less than 10

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:03.460
milliseconds.

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:08.760
And like I said, speed
has a quality all its own

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:10.740
because you can deliver
insights faster,

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:13.290
you can apply later
transformations

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:15.900
at incredible scale, you
can do things in sequence

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:18.660
that if you start out year
over some several seconds,

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:21.540
it's very hard to make
things fast later.

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:23.400
How fast is 10 milliseconds?

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:25.720
It's about 50 times faster
than you blink your eye,

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:26.490
and it's about--

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:30.380
if anybody remembers what
analog cameras looked like--

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:35.705
a 1/60th shutter is
about 10 milliseconds.

00:19:35.705 --> 00:19:36.330
So pretty fast.

00:19:38.890 --> 00:19:41.350
So I want to spend just
a couple of minutes

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:45.583
on our high-level diagram,
and before I start this,

00:19:45.583 --> 00:19:48.250
we are in the process of writing
a white paper and open sourcing

00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:51.640
many of not just the
architecture but the code

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:53.290
we use to do this.

00:19:53.290 --> 00:19:55.760
So obviously feel free to
take a picture of your phone,

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:57.910
but over the summer we're
going to be open sourcing

00:19:57.910 --> 00:19:59.880
this somewhere to follow.

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:02.160
And we view this as the
start of a discussion,

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:03.967
not the end of a discussion.

00:20:03.967 --> 00:20:06.550
And for those of you that have
seen some of these other booths

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:08.770
outside, you'll recognize
a lot of these tools.

00:20:08.770 --> 00:20:12.790
I want to start with number one,
orienting you to my diagram.

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:15.460
On the lower left, we
have an abstraction

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:18.940
for our on premise data centers,
so I'm going to start there.

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:21.970
Starting for the lower left
where that 1 just appeared,

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:24.310
we have log collectors
and multiple data centers.

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:25.480
We scale them horizontally.

00:20:25.480 --> 00:20:27.520
We have internal load balancing.

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:30.513
And additionally we apply
BeyondCorp principles.

00:20:30.513 --> 00:20:31.930
These are denied
by default. These

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:35.140
are controlled by programmatic
regular key rotation

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:36.160
via service account.

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:38.340
They communicate only
via two-way SSL 443

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:39.540
over the internet.

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:41.890
They're accessible via
a fully qualified domain

00:20:41.890 --> 00:20:44.590
name internally and
externally, things like this.

00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:49.002
And additionally
we apply metadata.

00:20:49.002 --> 00:20:51.210
One of the key things about
a published and subscribe

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:52.530
framework like this is you--

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:54.420
thou shalt apply metadata.

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:58.175
We apply metadata about as
many things as we can think of.

00:20:58.175 --> 00:20:59.550
And we also have
a framework that

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:01.440
allows us to change
the kinds of metadata

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:04.050
that we apply without
fundamentally re-architecting

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:07.410
our system or taking the
system offline to do that.

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:10.650
That's because we have this
load balance configuration,

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:12.720
and some of the SRE
principles you probably

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:14.340
heard about in the
Five Nines Lounge,

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:17.670
we apply throughout
this pipeline.

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:20.520
And, two, we have what we
call cloud-to-cloud data.

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:23.470
As I said, the city has
a hybrid cloud strategy.

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:25.773
So we via API and
service accounts

00:21:25.773 --> 00:21:27.315
using the same
BeyondCorp principles,

00:21:27.315 --> 00:21:30.900
key rotation, et cetera,
extract that data via API

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:33.850
from multiple public cloud
providers including Google,

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:35.620
including others.

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:38.100
And that's because we want
to leverage the investments

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:39.720
that the city has
made in these clouds

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:42.900
to provide cutting edge
services to residents

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:47.280
and introduce them
into our pipeline.

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:51.120
And number 3, the middle
part of the slide, we

00:21:51.120 --> 00:21:55.290
have a ingestion front end.

00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:56.880
It's a Pub/Sub.

00:21:56.880 --> 00:21:58.295
As you might have--

00:21:58.295 --> 00:21:59.670
you could probably
guess, it uses

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:02.430
a topic in subscription schema.

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:04.530
This topic in
subscription schema

00:22:04.530 --> 00:22:08.430
is one that is primarily
used later in our pipeline

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:11.170
to parse and prepare
logs for consumers,

00:22:11.170 --> 00:22:13.110
which I'll get to in a second.

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:14.880
And so there's a front end.

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:18.000
We call that ingest right
where to the left of the three,

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:19.620
and what it does
is it takes in data

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:22.110
from all of these places
from our data centers

00:22:22.110 --> 00:22:24.960
from our other public
cloud providers.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:26.820
And it adds it to
the right topic.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:29.190
It applies a little
bit more metadata.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:31.980
And it passes it off to
something we call flow control.

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:33.990
Flow control is an
Apache Beam pipeline,

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:36.420
which ensures that the data
is in the right format,

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:39.150
that the data is
expected, and really it's

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:41.730
a place that we can
apply metadata level

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:48.310
automation and normalization
across this data without--

00:22:48.310 --> 00:22:51.570
in nerd speak--
deserializing these packets.

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:53.840
And that's like I said
because we want it to be fast

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:56.130
because we want
and because we do

00:22:56.130 --> 00:22:58.370
use multiple
concurrent topics, we

00:22:58.370 --> 00:23:02.570
want to be able to serve the
data as fast as our fastest

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:06.530
consumer wants to consume it
rather than be limited by as

00:23:06.530 --> 00:23:08.473
slow as our slowest process.

00:23:08.473 --> 00:23:09.890
So to do that,
that is why we have

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:13.790
to Apache Beam subscriptions
if you will instead of one

00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:18.302
like some of the other pipelines
you might see around the hall.

00:23:18.302 --> 00:23:19.760
I also want to
point your attention

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:22.990
to the-- there's a blue
box which is our primary,

00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:25.880
and there's also a gray
box which is replica.

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:27.560
And so we have--

00:23:27.560 --> 00:23:30.550
we rely on Google managed
services, which provide

00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:32.490
a high level of reliability.

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:36.380
But we via our infrastructure
as code and other processes

00:23:36.380 --> 00:23:41.270
replicate this pipeline to
other geographic locations,

00:23:41.270 --> 00:23:44.780
and that's to ensure continuity
and disaster recovery.

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:47.480
And we don't, quote, operate
two separate infrastructures

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:49.850
via infrastructure as code.

00:23:49.850 --> 00:23:53.110
We simply tell Google put
this over there as well,

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:55.740
and I'll get into that also.

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:59.180
And number 4, we have
another Apache Beam pipeline

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:00.470
which parses data.

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:01.970
And broadly speaking,
our consumers

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:03.270
want a variety of things.

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:06.710
I mean consumers, I mean
analytical processes and tools.

00:24:06.710 --> 00:24:08.480
Some analytical
processes and tools

00:24:08.480 --> 00:24:09.980
want data to be normalized.

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:11.940
They just want you
to make sure it's--

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:15.060
they just want a well-formatted
JSON message and they're happy.

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:18.020
Some of them want the
data to be fully indexed.

00:24:18.020 --> 00:24:21.620
They want it to be transformed
into a CSV or an [? AVRO ?]

00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:24.530
or another type of message
which has a high structure.

00:24:24.530 --> 00:24:27.560
Others want the data
to be fully digitized.

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:29.150
So you might imagine
that those are--

00:24:29.150 --> 00:24:31.233
some of those are relatively
straightforward to do

00:24:31.233 --> 00:24:33.320
and some of those are not.

00:24:33.320 --> 00:24:35.450
Because we use this publish
and subscribe framework

00:24:35.450 --> 00:24:38.030
because we have these
two message buses,

00:24:38.030 --> 00:24:41.090
these two Pub/Sub
sandwiches, if you will

00:24:41.090 --> 00:24:44.900
we can do that like I said and
serve our fastest customer now

00:24:44.900 --> 00:24:48.500
and our, quote, slowest
customer at their own speed.

00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:50.360
And that is, I think,
a key differentiator

00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:52.250
with our architecture,
and, like I said,

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:56.330
that enables us to be
fast and stay fast.

00:24:56.330 --> 00:24:59.180
Number 5, we call
this the replicator.

00:24:59.180 --> 00:25:01.428
We have a production and
development environment.

00:25:01.428 --> 00:25:03.470
And importantly about our
development environment

00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:04.928
is it's only there
when we want it.

00:25:04.928 --> 00:25:06.530
Obviously we're civil servants.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:08.498
These are taxpayer resources.

00:25:08.498 --> 00:25:10.040
In addition to
effectiveness, we need

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:11.780
to be ruthlessly efficient.

00:25:11.780 --> 00:25:14.880
So we don't have a separate
development environment

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:17.420
which is, quote, sitting
around waiting us for to--

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:19.130
waiting for us to develop.

00:25:19.130 --> 00:25:21.117
Via infrastructure
as code, we stand up

00:25:21.117 --> 00:25:22.200
a development environment.

00:25:22.200 --> 00:25:24.380
We want to push major changes.

00:25:24.380 --> 00:25:26.730
We want to do ATT&amp;CK
framework testing.

00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:28.910
Will we want to
test integrations

00:25:28.910 --> 00:25:30.980
and other services,
things like this,

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:32.900
and we do that via
the replicator.

00:25:32.900 --> 00:25:37.250
We have a variety of
projects ongoing at any one

00:25:37.250 --> 00:25:39.950
time, a variety of
stakeholders, which

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:43.640
want what we might call
R&amp;D or non-production level

00:25:43.640 --> 00:25:44.970
access to our data.

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:48.420
And this replicator enables
us to take some, all,

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:51.530
or some subset of our production
data, put it to these,

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:54.170
apply masking, apply
tokenization and other

00:25:54.170 --> 00:25:57.410
processes without affecting
these downstream consumers.

00:25:57.410 --> 00:25:59.600
And that allows us
to continue to apply

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:01.700
best practices BeyondCorp
type principles

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:05.103
throughout this
production pipeline.

00:26:05.103 --> 00:26:07.520
Moving right along, these are
just some of the consumers--

00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:10.160
automation orchestration,
dashboard and alerting,

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:10.970
data warehouse--

00:26:10.970 --> 00:26:12.595
I'm actually going
to-- during the demo

00:26:12.595 --> 00:26:15.500
I'll show you BigQuery, which
we use for our data warehouse.

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:18.020
But this is like I said just
one small part of a very

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:21.230
large production system.

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:24.200
Number 7, if you see is--

00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:27.830
in the middle top,
infrastructure as code

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:29.990
means that your
operators write software.

00:26:29.990 --> 00:26:35.390
Software requires CICD, code
repos, and artifact storage.

00:26:35.390 --> 00:26:37.010
So we have that.

00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:38.470
There's no secret.

00:26:38.470 --> 00:26:43.540
It's people writing software,
and like anything, it

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:46.030
requires maybe a
change of perspective

00:26:46.030 --> 00:26:48.730
or a change in the way your
operational teams might

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:49.840
think of themselves.

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:53.500
But we found that the engineers
and operators on our team

00:26:53.500 --> 00:26:55.820
have really embraced
this challenge,

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:59.080
so I think that it is a journey
that certainly doesn't happen

00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:01.960
overnight but one
that we've been

00:27:01.960 --> 00:27:04.120
found to be very successful.

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:05.810
And last, but
certainly not least,

00:27:05.810 --> 00:27:09.610
is we use the Identity Aware
Proxy to broker access from

00:27:09.610 --> 00:27:12.790
users to all of our production
applications that means that we

00:27:12.790 --> 00:27:15.160
have context to our
access with user identity,

00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:17.500
with device date,
with other things .

00:27:17.500 --> 00:27:21.430
And again this enables us to
get right data, right analysts,

00:27:21.430 --> 00:27:23.510
right now.

00:27:23.510 --> 00:27:25.630
So with that, demo.

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:38.700
