00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:56.330
and things like
that to prototype

00:29:56.330 --> 00:29:59.320
within our development
environment, which we then

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:01.680
normal software
development process promote

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:05.760
to production via our automation
orchestration framework.

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:10.820
So all of this is preamble
to maybe first we'll just--

00:30:10.820 --> 00:30:12.200
and this is just SQL.

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:15.213
Again nothing earth shattering,
just select from our demo,

00:30:15.213 --> 00:30:16.880
and make sure that
our message is there.

00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:35.570
You have to take my word for
it, but it is the same message.

00:30:35.570 --> 00:30:39.880
And you see so time, source IP,
source board, destination IP,

00:30:39.880 --> 00:30:41.290
right failure.

00:30:41.290 --> 00:30:44.280
So this, for example, could be
a malicious actor attempting

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:49.160
to SSH into a host that was
within our on premise data

00:30:49.160 --> 00:30:50.400
center.

00:30:50.400 --> 00:30:54.990
So let's say-- let's
take that as the example.

00:30:54.990 --> 00:30:56.442
And again this
would probably not

00:30:56.442 --> 00:30:57.900
be in the context
of a BigQuery UI.

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:00.930
It has to be in the context
of an alerting dashboard

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:01.980
and a response process.

00:31:05.312 --> 00:31:07.270
So obviously let's take
a look at this address,

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:08.620
the destination IP.

00:31:08.620 --> 00:31:10.780
So we receive an
alert about someone

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:14.360
trying to do something
bad to our hosts.

00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:17.590
Now let's see what else is
happening to this host briefly.

00:31:24.430 --> 00:31:29.500
So now we're just saying gimme
all the SSH logs from our host.

00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:32.080
So we see a bunch of
failures and undetermined,

00:31:32.080 --> 00:31:34.510
which is a different kind
of failure in SSH logs,

00:31:34.510 --> 00:31:35.110
undetermined.

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:38.050
We see a success.

00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:41.140
And I actually see a
lot a bunch of failures

00:31:41.140 --> 00:31:42.820
within the recent time frame.

00:31:42.820 --> 00:31:45.250
So this is 308, 302.

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:49.630
So these are all temporally
relatively recent failure.

00:31:49.630 --> 00:31:52.330
Success doesn't matter,
undetermined interesting.

00:31:52.330 --> 00:31:54.430
So let's say there's this
110 address, which we

00:31:54.430 --> 00:31:56.990
see a bunch of failures from.

00:31:56.990 --> 00:31:58.720
So now we have understood.

00:31:58.720 --> 00:32:00.010
We received an alert.

00:32:00.010 --> 00:32:02.210
Someone's trying to
SSH into this system.

00:32:02.210 --> 00:32:04.210
We look back-- let's
call it 10 minutes--

00:32:04.210 --> 00:32:06.430
and we see a bunch
of other failures

00:32:06.430 --> 00:32:09.880
not from this initial system
but from other systems trying

00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:11.290
to access the same system.

00:32:11.290 --> 00:32:14.700
So this could be a
brute force SSH attempt.

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:16.210
That's so this could be.

00:32:16.210 --> 00:32:21.795
Now let's just filter
by this 110 address

00:32:21.795 --> 00:32:23.920
and maybe see if we can
see anything else going on.

00:32:31.670 --> 00:32:37.950
Now all 22, 22, 22, failure.

00:32:37.950 --> 00:32:38.990
They're all failures.

00:32:38.990 --> 00:32:42.830
So now I'm more convinced still
that this is a brute force SSH

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:45.130
attempt.

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:49.670
Now let's see-- we have
just the SSH logs here.

00:32:49.670 --> 00:32:54.350
Let's pivot from SSH to
all the logs that we have.

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:57.220
Now for the purposes of
this demo, we have DNS,

00:32:57.220 --> 00:33:01.860
SSH, web server, logs,
but in, quote, real life,

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:04.148
you might have a number
of different log sources

00:33:04.148 --> 00:33:06.440
that would depend on how your
use cases and your threat

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:08.815
management team or whatever
your processes are developed.

00:33:08.815 --> 00:33:11.420
But for the purposes of
this demo, it's just a few.

00:33:18.650 --> 00:33:22.550
So we see more of these poor 22.

00:33:22.550 --> 00:33:24.510
Now we see a bunch of DNS.

00:33:24.510 --> 00:33:27.360
So we see 53 mail dot Google.

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:29.700
So we see a bunch of
other things going on.

00:33:29.700 --> 00:33:33.110
And at this point, this might
be the point at which this was

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:35.060
delivered to a human analyst.

00:33:35.060 --> 00:33:37.490
Everything up to now
could have been--

00:33:37.490 --> 00:33:39.620
you could-- you don't
have to necessarily

00:33:39.620 --> 00:33:40.730
have a person do it.

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:44.750
It's-- to prototype, you would
obviously have to design these

00:33:44.750 --> 00:33:48.020
queries, implement thresholds,
production, unit testing,

00:33:48.020 --> 00:33:52.640
smoke testing, again, no secrets
about how these processes would

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:53.790
have to be undertaken.

00:33:53.790 --> 00:33:58.130
But let's say brute forcing
SSH against a jump server

00:33:58.130 --> 00:34:00.200
were one of your use
cases, this might

00:34:00.200 --> 00:34:04.190
be the kind of contextual,
quote, advanced automation

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:07.516
you could do with a
pipeline like this.

00:34:07.516 --> 00:34:10.389
So a lot of the NSS--

00:34:10.389 --> 00:34:14.060
in SSH so let's
see other than this

00:34:14.060 --> 00:34:16.530
if they tried to connect
to anything else.

00:34:16.530 --> 00:34:19.040
So now I'm-- maybe
I'm a human analyst.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:21.170
I'm in my iPython
notebook, I'm sitting,

00:34:21.170 --> 00:34:24.199
and I say I have my source IP.

00:34:24.199 --> 00:34:26.690
I already know there's
a bunch of DNS and SSH,

00:34:26.690 --> 00:34:30.590
so I'm done looking at DNS and
SSH so let's look at the stuff.

00:34:33.389 --> 00:34:37.719
So now they have tried to
connect to something else.

00:34:37.719 --> 00:34:39.510
And this is a brand new system.

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:41.459
I didn't know about
this system before.

00:34:41.459 --> 00:34:43.969
It wasn't organic to
my alert, but I've

00:34:43.969 --> 00:34:45.860
pivoted from a brute force--

00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:49.260
an SSH alert that
was decorated that

00:34:49.260 --> 00:34:54.580
was enriched by our pipeline and
data within our data warehouse.

00:34:54.580 --> 00:34:56.730
I then served that
to an analyst, who

00:34:56.730 --> 00:35:00.660
then applied human machine
learning-- human thinking

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:02.910
and said, I'm interested
in this alert.

00:35:02.910 --> 00:35:05.380
Let's see if they try to
connect to anything else.

00:35:05.380 --> 00:35:07.530
And now I could do this
whole process again

00:35:07.530 --> 00:35:12.540
with this new indicator-- a
potential indicator of attack.

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:14.370
And again I wouldn't
necessarily have

00:35:14.370 --> 00:35:15.870
to run all these queries again.

00:35:15.870 --> 00:35:18.510
I could simply feed this
back into the system,

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:24.300
wait a couple milliseconds for
another decorated contextual

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:27.760
data element to appear to me as
a human analyst, and on we go.

00:35:27.760 --> 00:35:33.681
So that's the end of that demo
and now back to the slides.

00:35:33.681 --> 00:35:37.490
If you could switch
back to the other one.

00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:39.650
Great.

00:35:39.650 --> 00:35:44.230
So zero trust and zero touch,
I have just a couple of minutes

00:35:44.230 --> 00:35:46.750
before I want to leave
some time for questions.

00:35:46.750 --> 00:35:52.680
And zero touch
infrastructure as code

00:35:52.680 --> 00:35:56.480
and how do we deploy systems
today as an organization?

00:35:56.480 --> 00:35:58.790
Obviously the city is a
hybrid cloud provider.

00:35:58.790 --> 00:36:01.010
It has, like I
said, a long history

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:03.590
of computing excellence.

00:36:03.590 --> 00:36:05.720
Physical data centers
are here to say.

00:36:05.720 --> 00:36:07.640
Data gravity is an
important consideration

00:36:07.640 --> 00:36:10.910
in how applications are
designed and architected.

00:36:10.910 --> 00:36:14.750
We're dedicated to ensuring
that those systems are also

00:36:14.750 --> 00:36:16.340
secure and reliable.

00:36:16.340 --> 00:36:20.570
But like I said, we wanted
to implement a fully

00:36:20.570 --> 00:36:24.330
infrastructure as code, a
fully zero touch application.

00:36:24.330 --> 00:36:28.190
And that means that we had
to transition or enhance

00:36:28.190 --> 00:36:32.810
our operational processes from
those of pure IT operations

00:36:32.810 --> 00:36:37.250
to development operations,
DevOps, and SRE principles.

00:36:37.250 --> 00:36:39.410
That means that we
have peer review,

00:36:39.410 --> 00:36:42.440
all codes are pushed to
a central repo, reviewed

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:46.820
by a select group,
audit abilities, 100%,

00:36:46.820 --> 00:36:48.770
and our controls are
technically enforced.

00:36:48.770 --> 00:36:51.440
So we have obviously
robust policy documents

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:54.650
which delineate who can
make changes to what.

00:36:54.650 --> 00:36:56.610
But don't just take
my word for it.

00:36:56.610 --> 00:37:00.380
We segment our environment
by project, by repository.

00:37:00.380 --> 00:37:05.170
And using Terraform and
open source CI/CD tools,

00:37:05.170 --> 00:37:07.940
we can technically
enforce our policies.

00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:11.170
And again, one of the principles
that I saw and I read--

00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:14.320
I responded to is we
don't want to blame people

00:37:14.320 --> 00:37:15.560
when something fails.

00:37:15.560 --> 00:37:17.890
We want to blame the system.

00:37:17.890 --> 00:37:21.790
So we wanted to construct
a system to allow people,

00:37:21.790 --> 00:37:24.250
knowing that they are
fallible, to receive

00:37:24.250 --> 00:37:28.630
the maximum technical
enforcement of our already

00:37:28.630 --> 00:37:30.850
agreed upon principles as
an engineering culture.

00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:35.105
And that means that--

00:37:35.105 --> 00:37:38.840
and some of those things started
with infrastructure as code,

00:37:38.840 --> 00:37:41.630
but they go to
good peer reviews.

00:37:41.630 --> 00:37:42.890
They go to lunch and learns.

00:37:42.890 --> 00:37:47.310
They go to a robust blameless
post-mortem culture.

00:37:47.310 --> 00:37:50.240
And if you don't
have these principles

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:53.540
and you don't technically
enforce these tools, at least

00:37:53.540 --> 00:37:57.530
in our experience, it's easy
to enhance a system in which it

00:37:57.530 --> 00:37:59.930
is accepted that people are
human beings and fallible

00:37:59.930 --> 00:38:01.910
and that we seek to
technically enforce things

00:38:01.910 --> 00:38:04.340
that we all agree on
rather than saying

00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:07.432
you should be more careful.

00:38:07.432 --> 00:38:09.640
And infrastructure as code
again, there's no secrets.

00:38:09.640 --> 00:38:12.490
This is actually part of
the code that runs the demo.

00:38:12.490 --> 00:38:15.560
And you see that these are,
if any of you use Kafka--

00:38:15.560 --> 00:38:17.390
if you haven't
used Cloud Pub/Sub

00:38:17.390 --> 00:38:21.240
and you have used Kafka,
these look like Kafka topics

00:38:21.240 --> 00:38:22.990
because these are Cloud
Pub/Sub subtopics,

00:38:22.990 --> 00:38:24.448
and that's basically
what they are.

00:38:24.448 --> 00:38:26.140
So we adjust third party.

00:38:26.140 --> 00:38:29.210
We do things flow control.

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:32.045
So again secret is
there's no secret.

00:38:32.045 --> 00:38:33.920
And again, because we're
using infrastructure

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:37.150
as code because we have
in the documentation,

00:38:37.150 --> 00:38:38.523
we might say that--

00:38:38.523 --> 00:38:40.690
if I were reviewing Andrew's
code, I'd say I would--

00:38:40.690 --> 00:38:42.580
I want more documentation
on your code.

00:38:42.580 --> 00:38:44.860
It's not really clear to me
what these variables are.

00:38:44.860 --> 00:38:48.560
Well, let's pretend that
as part of our code review,

00:38:48.560 --> 00:38:51.460
we can ensure that our
infrastructure as code

00:38:51.460 --> 00:38:53.650
is well documented
because we're not

00:38:53.650 --> 00:38:55.600
at some different
point in time taking

00:38:55.600 --> 00:38:59.260
some snapshot of our environment
and then documenting it.

00:38:59.260 --> 00:39:02.070
This is the documentation
of our environment right

00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:04.830
here, the
infrastructure as code.

00:39:04.830 --> 00:39:07.330
And like I said, we have a
full development and testing

00:39:07.330 --> 00:39:07.830
environment.

00:39:07.830 --> 00:39:10.860
We have geographically
redundant components

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:14.698
for critical systems,
and we don't do that

00:39:14.698 --> 00:39:15.990
by clicking a bunch of buttons.

00:39:15.990 --> 00:39:19.482
We do that by introducing
variables into our Terraform

00:39:19.482 --> 00:39:20.690
code-- which is what this is.

00:39:20.690 --> 00:39:26.388
This is HCL-- and having
the computer do it for us.

00:39:26.388 --> 00:39:27.930
And zero trust--
and for those of you

00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:29.490
that haven't heard
about BeyondCorp

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:32.990
or haven't read the white
papers from Forrester Research

00:39:32.990 --> 00:39:35.730
or by Google themselves
and BeyondCorp,

00:39:35.730 --> 00:39:37.590
we remove the trust
in the network

00:39:37.590 --> 00:39:41.640
and place it in what we know
about the user, the device,

00:39:41.640 --> 00:39:43.230
and the context.

00:39:43.230 --> 00:39:45.030
Our pipeline uses
these principles

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:48.810
again throughout the pipeline to
ensure that our environment is

00:39:48.810 --> 00:39:51.120
secure and because
we believe that this

00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:53.370
is the future way
that applications

00:39:53.370 --> 00:39:54.600
will be architected.

00:39:54.600 --> 00:39:57.340
And again this is going
to happen overnight.

00:39:57.340 --> 00:40:01.950
The city has been a city
actually about 120 years longer

00:40:01.950 --> 00:40:04.380
than the United States of
America has been a country.

00:40:04.380 --> 00:40:06.060
So the city as an
organization is

00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:10.020
one with a rich
history of transition

00:40:10.020 --> 00:40:11.830
and using inflection points.

00:40:11.830 --> 00:40:14.220
And we're confident
that the city is going

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:16.720
to move in this direction.

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:18.480
And we're excited to
be a very small part

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:22.290
of that team of teams like I
said that's going to do it.

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:24.030
But you have to start someplace.

00:40:24.030 --> 00:40:27.890
And for those in the audience,
I want to do infrastructure

00:40:27.890 --> 00:40:30.540
as code, I want
to try BeyondCorp,

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:33.510
this is a big,
important application.

00:40:33.510 --> 00:40:36.060
But it's one in
which we deliberately

00:40:36.060 --> 00:40:39.510
selected to apply both
of these principles

00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:41.665
not just because we believe
in those principles,

00:40:41.665 --> 00:40:44.040
we think that the future, and
we think they're important,

00:40:44.040 --> 00:40:49.380
but this was small enough to be
accomplishable but big enough

00:40:49.380 --> 00:40:50.220
to matter.

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:53.400
And I think selecting the
kind of problem or project

00:40:53.400 --> 00:40:57.052
you as an organization
apply these cutting edge

00:40:57.052 --> 00:40:59.010
next generation or whatever
you want to call it

00:40:59.010 --> 00:41:02.120
principles for is important.

00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:05.240
And moving right along
as another way of analogy

00:41:05.240 --> 00:41:07.610
because "Game of
Thrones" is restarting,

00:41:07.610 --> 00:41:10.580
we wanted to put a
picture of a castle in.

00:41:10.580 --> 00:41:11.790
And so the analogy is simple.

00:41:11.790 --> 00:41:13.610
We have a castle.

00:41:13.610 --> 00:41:18.260
We have old, we have legacy,
we have all these systems,

00:41:18.260 --> 00:41:20.130
and we need to do
two things at once.

00:41:20.130 --> 00:41:21.588
We need to chew
bubblegum and walk.

00:41:21.588 --> 00:41:24.590
We need to embrace next
generation technologies

00:41:24.590 --> 00:41:26.510
and novel approaches
to solving problems

00:41:26.510 --> 00:41:30.500
while ensuring that our existing
systems are robust and secure.

00:41:30.500 --> 00:41:33.410
And since the adoption
of the internet,

00:41:33.410 --> 00:41:37.160
many organizations have tried
and succeeded in doing this,

00:41:37.160 --> 00:41:38.870
and we think that
our organization

00:41:38.870 --> 00:41:40.620
with the help of Google
and other partners

00:41:40.620 --> 00:41:43.370
is going to succeed as well
and that your organization

00:41:43.370 --> 00:41:46.543
hopefully can, too.

00:41:46.543 --> 00:41:48.960
And the future, just a couple
of minutes, machine learning

00:41:48.960 --> 00:41:49.800
and advanced automation.

00:41:49.800 --> 00:41:51.090
Like I said at the
beginning, people

00:41:51.090 --> 00:41:52.030
don't want machine learning.

00:41:52.030 --> 00:41:54.530
They want learning, and they
don't want advanced automation.

00:41:54.530 --> 00:41:57.270
They want the thing they want
done to be done really fast.

00:41:57.270 --> 00:42:01.230
So a data centric,
automation focused approach

00:42:01.230 --> 00:42:05.190
like that you saw
here enables that.

00:42:05.190 --> 00:42:07.260
And centaur chests, what we're--

00:42:07.260 --> 00:42:08.970
our vision for
what we want to do

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:12.892
isn't to hire a bunch of robots
to replace a bunch of people.

00:42:12.892 --> 00:42:14.100
That's never going to happen.

00:42:14.100 --> 00:42:15.267
What we want to do--

00:42:15.267 --> 00:42:17.100
and if you've never
heard of centaur chests,

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:21.330
it's a person un-personed
or a person machine team

00:42:21.330 --> 00:42:24.810
a computer applies the
strategy and tactics

00:42:24.810 --> 00:42:26.380
of its human operator.

00:42:26.380 --> 00:42:30.990
So using a person to think
strategically to draw analogy

00:42:30.990 --> 00:42:34.530
to make plans and designs
and then having a system

00:42:34.530 --> 00:42:37.020
execute those at high
velocity, high reliability,

00:42:37.020 --> 00:42:38.190
and high scale.

00:42:38.190 --> 00:42:39.540
That's what we're trying to do.

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:42.612
Training-- machine learning pun
intended-- people and machines

00:42:42.612 --> 00:42:43.320
to work together.

00:42:46.160 --> 00:42:48.090
And last but not
least, key takeaways.

00:42:48.090 --> 00:42:51.250
One, the benefit of using the
public cloud for this purpose--

00:42:51.250 --> 00:42:54.520
scalability, performance,
efficiency, and the ability

00:42:54.520 --> 00:42:56.770
to technology leapfrog.

00:42:56.770 --> 00:42:59.740
We selected a brand new
application that was important,

00:42:59.740 --> 00:43:02.320
executive sponsorship,
big impact,

00:43:02.320 --> 00:43:05.920
and we sought to apply
these principles on purpose.

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:10.090
Two, our approach zero trust,
zero touch, and the power

00:43:10.090 --> 00:43:13.090
of data, which broadly
speaking one of the primary--

00:43:13.090 --> 00:43:15.970
the first things we did was
robust retrospective search

00:43:15.970 --> 00:43:18.130
but that's just the beginning.

00:43:18.130 --> 00:43:22.335
And number 3, enable the
future today with automation,

00:43:22.335 --> 00:43:27.100
machine learning, published
and subscribed frameworks.

00:43:27.100 --> 00:43:30.730
And number 4, not on the slide,
white paper coming this summer,

00:43:30.730 --> 00:43:33.400
hopefully we start a
conversation with all of you.

00:43:33.400 --> 00:43:35.920
Follow us on Twitter, New
York City Cyber Command.

00:43:35.920 --> 00:43:40.900
Questions about any of this, we
have a contact email up there.

00:43:40.900 --> 00:43:43.320
And for any information
about our office, about us,

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:47.206
about what we do, about our
programs, nyc.gov/cyber.

00:43:47.206 --> 00:43:48.950
And that's it.

00:43:48.950 --> 00:43:52.000
[APPLAUSE]

